  "horizon": {
    "why": "THERE IS NO WATER ACTOR. The flat blue expanse in cp05b-04 is the SkyAtmosphere's virtual planet ground shell, seen below the true horizon where the JerusalemContext terrain mesh runs out. That terrain is a 6.4 km square (the DEM extent below); from the frame-04 camera the forward ray leaves it at about 5.95 km, and every screen ray within roughly 3.8 degrees below the horizon then lands on the sky shell. ExponentialHeightFog with fogMaxOpacity 0.85 leaves 15 per cent of that shell showing at any distance, and SkyAtmosphere aerialPerspectiveViewDistanceScale 1.8 blues out the last kilometres of real terrain until its silhouette reads as a coastline. Saturated blue field, razor-straight upper edge, terrain-shaped lower edge: a sea.",
    "ruledOut": "Scripts/release_water.py places thirteen Yechezkel 47 meshes plus RELEASE_Water_Driver, all inside the courts and the four mikvaot, and its own limitation note says the stream stops 4000 amot east of the outer gate and the Dead Sea is asserted, not depicted. A string scan of both .umap files finds no WaterBody, BP_Sky_Sphere, Ocean, Backdrop, Horizon, SkyDome, Landscape or FarField reference, and the largest XY scale of any actor in the level is REVIEW_KotelPhoto_0 at about 40 m by 20 m. Nothing to remove, nothing to resize.",
    "perStateNote": "The per-state hiding mechanism is AMikdashEnclosure's HideWhileWallStandsTags / HideWhileModernCityStandsTags (V key, EMikdashPrecinctState). It is NOT the lever here: SkyAtmosphere_0 cannot be hidden per state without removing the sky itself.",
    "actionNote": "Extending the terrain to the Dead Sea is the real fix and is far out of scope for one pass. The mitigation is two numbers, both read back before they are written and both recorded with their previous value so --revert restores them exactly. Raising fogMaxOpacity to 1.0 makes the far background resolve fully to the fog colour - which is the sky atmosphere ambient, not a saturated planet blue - so the shell stops reading as water. Bringing aerialPerspectiveViewDistanceScale back to 1.0 stops the last kilometres of real terrain dissolving into that band, so the silhouette reads as hills rather than a coastline. Near and mid field are governed by the exponential height falloff and are not touched by either number.",
    "giantFlatActorTest": "flat (Z extent under 2 per cent of the larger XY extent), larger than a quarter of the DEM span, and reaching beyond the DEM the terrain mesh was generated from",
